-- A tool that is out cannot be borrowed. `Borrow` is a transient act, so
-- `never (Borrow where ...)` is a refusal: Reference §21 says a refusal "is checked once, when
-- the request arrives". `returnedOn` is set by rule TakeBackTool. `velle validate` refuses it:
--
--   - [V10] a 'never' reads 'Loan.returnedOn', which rule 'TakeBackTool' assigns, so it is a guarantee that the rules keep it true, and the compiler cannot yet prove one over every assignment; read a field no rule assigns, or derive 'returnedOn' from the records the rule writes (Reference §21)
--
-- The message calls a refusal a guarantee. Reference §21 says a guarantee "is a prohibition over
-- state that only rules can produce", proved at compile time, and that nobody is ever refused by
-- one. This `never` turns away a Borrow when it arrives and binds no rule. A reader would expect
-- it to validate, or else a message that says why a refusal that reads an assigned field is
-- refused. V10 refusing a `never` over an assigned field is OQ53's item "Prove a `never` over a
-- rule-written field inductively", shown over a kept act in `03-assigned-field-refused.velle`.
-- The word "guarantee" for a refusal is not in OQ53.
act Tool { name: text }

transient act Borrow { tool: one Tool }

shape Loan {
    tool: one Tool
    returnedOn: Date?
}

rule LendTool when Borrow {
    Loan from { tool: tool, returnedOn: none }
}

transient act ReturnTool { loan: one Loan }

rule TakeBackTool when ReturnTool {
    loan.returnedOn = today
}

never (Borrow where exists (tool.loans where returnedOn is none))
